<!DOCTYPE flight_plan SYSTEM "../flight_plan.dtd">

<!-- =====================================================================================
     IMAV 2026 outdoor Mission 4 (rulebook 5.4.7) - "Dead man" first-aid kit drop
     Aircraft: Multiplex EasyStar 3 with MORA companion computer (CATIA, EARcam microphone)
     =====================================================================================

     THE TASK
       Three mannequins lie within 25 m of a GPS point the organisers give on the day.
       One wears an MSA motionSCOUT K-T-R alarm (2.6-3.0 kHz, 95 dB at 3 m). Drop the
       first-aid kit on THAT mannequin.

     THE RULES THAT SHAPE THIS PLAN
       - Points only when the kit is released BELOW 2 m above ground (judged from the ground).
       - Full points within 50 cm of the navel, none beyond 300 cm; doubled on the alarm one.
       - Everything must be autonomous for autonomy factor 1.0 (no GCS clicks after start).
       - Max flight altitude 80 m AGL; kill switch on the RC; safety pilot present.

     THE SITE (IGN LiDAR HD, 50 cm, https://data.geopf.fr/altimetrie; SRTM is far too coarse)
       The GROUND around M4C is flat: 142.5 m MSL, within 1 m over 600 m. What looks like a
       ridge west of the field in Google Earth is FOREST: 12 to 18 m trees whose edge runs
       north-south only 20 to 25 m WEST of M4C. A tree row of 8 to 14 m stands 25 to 50 m
       EAST of M4C (y -50 .. +45), joined to a hedge further north-east, and a tree block
       closes the meadow 210 m NORTH of M4C. The meadow is open to the SOUTH and SOUTH-EAST.
       So the mannequins lie in a 45 m wide north-south lane between trees. The obstacles
       are in this plan as sectors M4_OW (west forest), M4_OE (east trees) and M4_ON (north
       block); the GCS draws them. Everything low is decided against them:
       - all circling and all search legs are flown at 45 m: 27 m above the tallest tree, so the
         motor-off glide of a search leg (about 13 m) still leaves 14 m,
       - the release run-in is flown SOUTH to NORTH along the lane (course 0, tilted by at
         most 20 deg if that keeps the corridor clear), START 300 m south at 15 m,
       - if no corridor 8 m clear of the tree crowns exists (target within about 8 m of a tree
         line) there is NO low release: the aircraft climbs to 45 m and holds in Standby, the
         team decides (a release above 2 m scores nothing, so it is not attempted),
       - the climb-out continues straight north in the lane as far as the geofence allows,
         then a climbing right-hand circle over the meadow before heading to Standby, and
       - a safety net: below 25 m inside an obstacle sector at any moment = motor on, climb.

     THE GEOFENCE
       Sector M4_FENCE is the hard geofence of the competition (corners GF1..GF4 of
       easystar_3_mora_camera_demo.xml). It is the Paparazzi geofence_sector: the autopilot
       switches to HOME mode the moment the aircraft leaves it; geofence_max_alt 220 m is
       80 m above the field. Its north edge runs only 142 m north of M4C, which is what
       limits the climb-out: earcam_place_climbout puts _CLIMBOUT as far north as a turn of
       M4_EXIT_TURN_RADIUS_M plus M4_FENCE_MARGIN_M stays inside the fence, and the run-in corridor test
       treats "outside the fence" like a tree. The star (about 110 m around the estimate) fits with 25 m to
       spare; do not enlarge earcam_refine_turn_radius_m here.

     CLIMB PERFORMANCE (>>> UPDATE FROM REAL FLIGHTS <<<)
       The climb-out has at most about 95 m (target in the north of the circle) to get above
       the 18 m trees before the exit turn. M4_CLIMBOUT_RATE_MPS / M4_CLIMBOUT_AIRSPEED_MPS
       below are what the plan asks for (3 m/s at 11 m/s = 27 %, i.e. 25 m in 95 m); the
       airframe default limit of 2 m/s is raised for that block only. Put the measured
       EasyStar 3 numbers here (flaperons for a slow steep climb are a further option once
       the airframe has a flap command and it is flight tested).

     THE PLAN IN THREE STEPS
       0. WIND (block m4_wind): one full circle over M4C at 45 m with the motor on. The
          wind_circle module fits the GPS ground speed vectors of that turn to a circle
          (same method as the ground station wind estimator, but ONBOARD - no datalink
          needed). Wind speed and direction are then known to nav_drop (release point) and
          used to choose, among the obstacle-free run-in courses, the one most into the wind.
       1. SEARCH (blocks m4_search .. refine_decide): fly a "star" of straight legs through
          the given point at 45 m. On each leg the motor is switched OFF around the centre so
          the microphone hears the alarm and not the pusher propeller. MORA fuses all quiet
          samples into the loudest spot; the next star is centred on that spot. Stop when
          two successive estimates agree within 1.5 m (at most 3 stars).
       2. RELEASE (blocks goto_loudspot .. drop_decide): the standard Paparazzi nav_drop module
          lays out a base turn, a 300 m straight run-in and the RELEASE point (wind, speed
          and fall height compensated). The run-in glides from 15 m to 1.5 m, holds 1.5 m on
          the rangefinder and opens the hatch the instant the RELEASE point is crossed, but
          ONLY if the aircraft is at or below 2 m. Otherwise: climb out, try again (max 3).
       3. LAND (blocks land .. flare): standard glide landing AF -> TD.

     BEFORE THE FLIGHT - THINGS YOU MUST ADJUST
       - Move waypoint M4C onto the point given by the organisers (GCS: drag it, or edit here).
         If it moves by more than a few metres, re-check the obstacle sectors and the lane
         course (M4_LANE_COURSE_DEG below) against the trees.
       - Check HOME / STDBY / CLIMB / AF / TD for the actual field, and ground_alt.
       - The wind is measured onboard in block m4_wind (one circle over M4C). Nothing to
         do, but in the GCS you can compare WIND_INFO_RET (from the aircraft) with the
         ground station's own wind estimate as a sanity check.
       - TRIGGER_DELAY (airframe, nav_drop section): measured time from hatch command to kit
         free, PLUS the GPS position latency. Every 0.1 s error is 1 m along track at 10 m/s.
       - Heights: the star legs (45 m) clear the trees; the release (1.5 m) is only flown in
         the lane. Do not lower the star height at this site.

     WHAT MUST BE ENABLED
       Modules digital_cam_uart, digital_cam_earcam (EARCAM_USE_AGL_DIST, EARCAM_USE_NAV_DROP),
       nav_drop (NAV_DROP_USE_AGL_DIST, NAV_DROP_RELEASE_WITH_DELAY, kit ALPHA/MASS), agl_dist
       with the VB22A rangefinder, wind_circle (loaded by this plan); CATIA on MORA started
       with "earcam" and "earcam-band 2400,3200" (see catia.service).

     HOW TO READ THE BLOCKS
       Paparazzi evaluates a block's <exception> lines BEFORE its body, every navigation cycle
       (20 Hz). "deroute" jumps to another block. A "pre_call" runs every cycle while the block
       is active. Waypoints starting with "_" are scratch: the code moves them, do not edit.
     ===================================================================================== -->
<flight_plan alt="200" ground_alt="140" lat0="48.808252" lon0="7.856789" max_dist_from_home="800" name="IMAV2026_M4_EARCAM" security_height="30" home_mode_height="70" geofence_sector="M4_FENCE" geofence_max_alt="220">
